---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const gcdCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'gcd(48, 18)?', note: 'The largest integer dividing both. Listing divisors works; Euclid\'s algorithm (Elements, ~300 BCE) is faster and always works.' },
    { math: '48 = 2 × 18 + 12', note: 'Divide and keep the remainder. Any common divisor of 48 and 18 must also divide 12.' },
    { math: '18 = 1 × 12 + 6', note: 'Repeat: the problem shrinks — gcd(18, 12) = gcd(48, 18).' },
    { math: '12 = 2 × 6 + 0 → gcd = 6', note: 'A zero remainder means the last divisor is the answer. Two millennia old, and still the GCD routine inside your computer.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/advanced/number-theory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Number theory studies the integers in all their stubbornness: primes, divisibility,
    remainders. It is mathematics at its purest — "useless" for centuries, then suddenly the
    foundation of every encrypted transaction on Earth. The gap between how simple the questions
    sound ("are there infinitely many twin primes?") and how hard they are to settle is the
    field's peculiar charm.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Euclid's algorithm — the world's oldest surviving algorithm, still in your CPU" widget="equationStep" config={gcdCfg} />

  <h2>The pillars</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fundamental theorem of arithmetic:</strong> every integer > 1 factors into primes in exactly one way. Primes are the atoms of multiplication.</li>
    <li><strong>Infinitely many primes:</strong> Euclid's contradiction proof — assume a complete list, multiply them all, add 1, and the new number is divisible by none of them.</li>
    <li><strong>Modular arithmetic:</strong> clocks, calendars and checksums — "12 + 5 = 5 (mod 12)". Gauss systematised it in 1801, and modern cryptography is modular arithmetic with very large numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>Density result:</strong> the prime number theorem (1896) says primes thin out predictably — about n/ln n of them below n. That regularity inside chaos is what RSA's security rests on.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it went from "pure" to "critical"</h2>
  <p>
    In 1977, Rivest, Shamir and Adleman built encryption from one-way arithmetic: multiplying
    two large primes is easy; factoring the product is (believed) hard. G.H. Hardy had proudly
    called number theory beautifully useless in 1940. Every HTTPS padlock in your browser is a
    postscript.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Euclid, Elements; Gauss, Disquisitiones Arithmeticae (1801); Rivest, Shamir & Adleman, CACM (1978).</p>
</TopicPage>
